```

Visit <http://localhost:8000/admin/> and sign in with the account you created.

Stop the local server with
Ctrl-C.{% if database != "sqlite3" %} Run `make stop` when you also want to stop
the database container.{% endif %} Run `make help` to see all available
development commands.

## Docker setup

{% if database == "sqlite3" %}Copy the example environment file to change the forwarded web port, then start
the complete development environment:
{%- else %}Copy the example environment file to change credentials or forwarded ports, then
start the complete development environment:
{%- endif %}

```shell
cp .env.example .env
make docker
```

`make docker` refreshes the floating `python:{{ python_version }}-slim`
development image before starting the services. This keeps Docker on the same
Python major/minor line while allowing patch updates.

Compose {% if database != "sqlite3" %}waits for {{ database_name }}, {% endif %}applies migrations and serves
Wagtail at <http://localhost:8000>. Create an administrator in another terminal:

```shell
docker compose exec web python manage.py createsuperuser
```

Stop the services with `make stop`.{% if database != "sqlite3" %} Run
`docker compose down --volumes` to also delete the local database data.{% endif %}
{%- if database == "mysql" %}

> [!NOTE]
> Django warns that MySQL cannot enforce Wagtail's conditional `WorkflowState`
> uniqueness constraint. This is a database limitation rather than a setup error.
{%- endif %}

## Local UV setup

{% if database == "sqlite3" %}Install the locked dependencies, migrate, and run Django locally:
{%- else %}Start only the database in Docker, install the locked dependencies, migrate,
and run Django locally:
{%- endif %}

```shell
make dev
```

For a new site, run `make superuser` in another terminal from the project root,
then sign in at <http://localhost:8000/admin/>.

{% if database == "sqlite3" %}SQLite data is stored in `db.sqlite3` in the project root.
{%- else %}The settings default to the database exposed on `127.0.0.1`. Override
`DATABASE_NAME`, `DATABASE_USER`, `DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_HOST`, and
`DATABASE_PORT` in `.env` when needed. Exported shell variables take precedence
for both local UV commands and Docker Compose. Use single quotes for literal
values containing dollar signs, hash characters, or spaces, for example:

```dotenv
DATABASE_PASSWORD='cost$5#demo with spaces'
```

Local Make targets load `.env` through UV when the file exists; otherwise Django
uses exported variables or its defaults. Compose reads `.env` itself and uses
`db` with the internal database port for its web service.

`make dev` starts the bundled database container. To use an existing external
database, configure its host, port, and credentials in `.env`, then run:

```shell
make sync migrate
uv run --env-file .env python manage.py runserver
```

`make superuser` and `make test` also honour those settings.
{%- endif %}

## Quality checks

AI agents default to running backend commands inside the Compose `web` service,
unless you choose the local UV workflow. The generated `AGENTS.md` links to focused guidance in
`docs/agent-instructions/`, including the development conventions and full
completion checklist.

For Docker development, build the image with `docker compose build`, then run:

```shell
docker compose run --rm web uv sync --locked
docker compose run --rm web uv lock --check
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff check .
docker compose run --rm web uv run ruff format --check .
docker compose run --rm web uv run python scripts/check_django_templates.py
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py check
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
docker compose run --rm web uv run python manage.py test
```

For local development, `make check` runs lint, formatting checks, and Django's
test suite. Also check the lockfile, system configuration, and missing migrations:

```shell
make check
uv lock --check
uv run{% if database != "sqlite3" %} --env-file .env{% endif %} python manage.py check
uv run{% if database != "sqlite3" %} --env-file .env{% endif %} python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run
```

{% if database != "sqlite3" %}For the bundled database, run `make database` first. Direct local Django
commands use `uv run --env-file .env` to load the same settings as Make targets.
Omit `--env-file .env` if all settings are supplied through exported variables
or defaults and no `.env` file exists.

{% endif %}The Git hooks below run on the host using UV, including during Docker development.

Initialize version control and install the hooks after generation. Pre-commit's
`--all-files` option means all files known to Git, so the initial `git add` is
required:

```shell
git init
git add --all
uv run pre-commit install
uv run pre-commit run --all-files
```

If a formatter changes files during that first run, inspect the changes and run
`git add --all` again before committing.

Use `uv add` and `uv remove` for dependencies. `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` are
authoritative; this project intentionally does not use `requirements.txt`.
